WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
[Informal Talk]

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Thank you. Thank you, Stephen. So what we are really going

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.590
to focus on here today are the issues that are going to be

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:21.129
most relevant to you graduating from Stanford and also

00:01:21.149 --> 00:01:25.180
starting up a startup company in the Silicon Valley and the

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.450
type of issues that you are going to face. And you will find

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.200
that the issues that you face as an entrepreneur starting a

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.659
small company are much different than you are going to face

00:01:34.679 --> 00:01:39.239
joining a big company. So if you find a job with Google, you

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:41.759
are probably not going to have very many immigration issues.

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:45.159
They have a very good staff there, and the immigration

00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:49.030
service really isn't looking to Google to scrutinize those

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:51.789
cases very much. But as an entrepreneur, unfortunately, it

00:01:51.809 --> 00:01:56.760
can also be very difficult in your situation, someone who is

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.929
graduating in F-1's status to secure work authorization

00:02:01.949 --> 00:02:05.810
after your one-year of OPT. So that's sort of the focus, but

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:09.079
we are also going to sort of just talk about some of the

00:02:09.079 --> 00:02:11.829
immigration basic. So you have an idea. Some of you might

00:02:11.829 --> 00:02:14.579
already know this but just some of the language that you

00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:17.329
need to know to understand how this process works. So we

00:02:17.329 --> 00:02:20.079
will talk about that, and then we will talk about initial

00:02:20.079 --> 00:02:22.329
concerns for startups and entrepreneurs. We will talk about

00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:25.079
temporary visas, and those are things that are going to be

00:02:25.079 --> 00:02:27.829
relevant to you once you graduate. Those are H-1Bs and the

00:02:27.829 --> 00:02:31.809
TNs and other nonimmigrant visas that will allow you to

00:02:31.829 --> 00:03:01.829
start working once your OPT expires. And we will also talk a

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:07.129
little bit about the interplay between your F-1 OPT and

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:08.879
changing status to a longer-term work authorized status like

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:09.879
the H-1B.

00:03:09.879 --> 00:03:12.629
And then we will spend very little time talking about Green

00:03:12.629 --> 00:03:14.629
Cards because that's probably not something that's that

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:17.629
relevant to you right now but just to give you a brief

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:20.379
overview so you know what that's about and what options are

00:03:20.379 --> 00:03:23.129
available to people like you or starting up companies in the

00:03:23.129 --> 00:03:25.879
United States. And then finally, just a minute or two on

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:27.879
proposed legislation to help entrepreneurs and startups, you

00:03:27.879 --> 00:03:30.629
might have heard of that there is so-called StartUp Visa Act

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:33.129
that was last year that unfortunately didn't go anywhere. We

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:36.379
will talk a little bit about that and what might be on the

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:38.129
horizon for proposed legislation in the future.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:40.879
Okay. So, I am just going to go through some basic

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:43.629
terminology so you understand this and not only one we go

00:03:43.629 --> 00:03:46.379
through the rest of presentation but when you are talking to

00:03:46.379 --> 00:03:48.629
potential employers or the Foreign Studies Office here, you

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:51.629
know what this all means. A nonimmigrant is someone who is a

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:53.879
foreign national coming to United States for a temporary

00:03:53.879 --> 00:03:57.129
purpose. So if you are all in F-1 student status, you are a

00:03:57.129 --> 00:03:59.129
nonimmigrant. You are coming here temporarily for the

00:03:59.129 --> 00:04:01.629
purposes of going to Stanford and learning. An immigrant is

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:03.879
someone that's coming to the United States for permanent

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:05.629
purposes. So that's someone who is planning on living here

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:06.629
permanently.

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:08.629
And traditionally, the nonimmigrants and the immigrants were

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:11.129
two very separate things, and you couldn't be a nonimmigrant

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:13.629
with the intention of becoming an immigrant, of living here

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:16.629
permanently. So you had to show, and many of you might have

00:04:16.629 --> 00:04:19.379
had to show this, when you applied for your F-1 your

00:04:19.379 --> 00:04:22.129
intention to go back to your home country, proof that you

00:04:22.129 --> 00:04:25.129
have ties in your home country that your family is going to

00:04:25.129 --> 00:04:27.879
be missing you. So that's traditionally how it works, and it

00:04:27.879 --> 00:04:30.379
still works for most visa classification. It works that way

00:04:30.379 --> 00:04:32.379
for the F-1. However, some nonimmigrant visa classifications

00:04:32.379 --> 00:04:34.379
are what we call "dual-intent classifications" and those

00:04:34.379 --> 00:04:37.379
include the H-1B and the L-1. And what that means is this

00:04:37.379 --> 00:04:40.300
that you can come on an F-1, when you apply for the F-1, you

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.800
have to show your intention of coming here temporarily. But

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.379
once you are here, you can change to H-1B and L-1 status,

00:04:46.399 --> 00:04:49.909
and once you are in H-1B and L-1 status, it's fine for your

00:04:49.909 --> 00:04:54.329
intention to live here permanently. So you don't face those

00:04:54.349 --> 00:04:57.750
restrictions. When you go to apply for a visa, an H-1B visa,

00:04:57.769 --> 00:05:00.060
they are not going to say, "Oh, are you planning on going

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:03.659
home to Germany?" They don't care with the H-1 or the L-1.

00:05:03.659 --> 00:05:05.659
So it's a good classification to be in.

00:05:05.659 --> 00:05:08.670
Audience

00:05:08.689 --> 00:05:12.030
The dual intent has been a de facto rule for a very long

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:15.759
time. Before I started practicing immigration law, it was

00:05:15.779 --> 00:05:19.269
actually added to the regulations and the statute I believe

00:05:19.289 --> 00:05:22.379
in 2000, or 1999 or 2000. So it's been around for a long

00:05:22.379 --> 00:05:25.870
time. But again, it not only applies to H-1B and L-1, and to

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:29.639
a lesser extent E-1, E-2 and O-1 which are classifications

00:05:29.659 --> 00:05:30.920
we will touch on.

00:05:30.939 --> 00:05:34.530
Finally, lawful permanent resident Green Card holder, this

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:36.810
is someone who has been granted permanent residency. They

00:05:36.829 --> 00:05:40.129
are living here permanently. They have no restrictions on

00:05:40.149 --> 00:05:42.019
where they work, what they do, they can do whatever they

00:05:42.019 --> 00:05:49.180
want so long as it's legal. So nonimmigrant visa - and this

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:51.490
is an important distinction between a nonimmigrant visa and

00:05:51.509 --> 00:05:54.800
arrival/departure record. Most of you, unless you are from

00:05:54.819 --> 00:05:58.089
Canada, are going to have an F-1 visa with your picture on

00:05:58.109 --> 00:06:01.680
it in your passport. That's a nonimmigrant visa. It's issued

00:06:01.699 --> 00:06:04.310
by the consular service abroad, either a consular or an

00:06:04.329 --> 00:06:08.120
embassy abroad. That allows you to enter the country. The

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:10.389
arrival/departure record is what Customs and Border

00:06:10.409 --> 00:06:13.449
Protection gives you at the port of entry when you enter the

00:06:13.469 --> 00:06:16.579
country, that little white piece of paper and it has a

00:06:16.599 --> 00:06:18.240
handwritten expiration date. Sometimes it's computer

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.449
generated. Once you are here, what's relevant is the

00:06:21.469 --> 00:06:24.170
arrival/departure record. That's what determines how long

00:06:24.189 --> 00:06:26.250
you can stay here.

00:06:26.269 --> 00:06:31.149
For F-1s, that period is D/S. They don't have an expiration

00:06:31.169 --> 00:06:34.079
date, which means that as long as you are a student, as long

00:06:34.079 --> 00:06:37.079
as you are enrolled in school and you have an I-20 that

00:06:37.079 --> 00:06:39.930
covers your period of studies, you are in valid status. But

00:06:39.949 --> 00:06:43.029
it's important to know this because sometimes people will

00:06:43.049 --> 00:06:47.160
say, "Oh, my visa is expiring," the thing in their passport

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.879
with a picture on it. That doesn't matter once you are here.

00:06:49.879 --> 00:06:52.379
Maybe it's issue for two months. Once you are here, what

00:06:52.379 --> 00:06:55.209
controls your period of authorized stay is the I-94. So when

00:06:55.209 --> 00:06:58.529
you switch to H-1B or L-1 or whatever you switch to, that's

00:06:58.529 --> 00:07:01.420
going to have an expiration date on it and that expiration

00:07:01.439 --> 00:07:04.060
date is very important. If you stay beyond that, then you

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:08.709
can find yourself in all sorts of trouble. But as an F-1

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:12.509
student, it's the D/S which means that you can stay here so

00:07:12.529 --> 00:07:15.819
long as you are you are enrolled in school and your I-20

00:07:15.839 --> 00:07:19.000
covers your studies. Do you have a question or --

00:07:19.019 --> 00:07:23.230
Audience

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:24.250
Yes, D/S.

00:07:23.739 --> 00:07:24.739
Audience

00:07:24.739 --> 00:07:35.029
Yeah, we can talk about that later maybe in the after

00:07:35.049 --> 00:07:40.579
session. Are there many people here on the J-1? Yeah, I mean

00:07:40.599 --> 00:07:44.219
it's very similar. It's the D/S, Duration of Status.

00:07:44.239 --> 00:07:49.969
Audience

00:07:49.989 --> 00:07:51.239
Once you enter the country?

00:07:50.989 --> 00:08:01.129
Audience

00:08:01.149 --> 00:08:04.709
That's a good way to put it, yeah. I mean it's complicated.

00:08:04.729 --> 00:08:10.529
But I bring this up mainly because when you do switch to a

00:08:10.549 --> 00:08:13.049
work authorized status, you are going to have an expiration

00:08:13.049 --> 00:08:16.049
date and it's very important to keep an eye on that because

00:08:16.049 --> 00:08:20.240
if you overstay that, then it's a problem. But yeah, as an

00:08:20.259 --> 00:08:23.669
F-1 or a J-1 or you have the duration of status, yeah, if

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:27.430
you stay beyond the I-20 which is issued for the F-1 or the

00:08:27.449 --> 00:08:33.750
IAP-66 which is issued for the - or the DS2019 which is

00:08:33.769 --> 00:08:37.340
issued for the J-1, that's also an issue but it's not as big

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.330
of an issue.

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:42.029
Next thing I want to mention is petition-based nonimmigrant

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:46.379
classification. What that is is usually work authorized

00:08:46.399 --> 00:08:51.750
classifications which require usually an employer to file a

00:08:51.769 --> 00:08:54.720
petition with the immigration service, get that petition

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:58.330
approved before you can get that nonimmigrant status. So

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.959
most of you are in F-1 status, that's not a petition-based

00:09:00.959 --> 00:09:04.669
nonimmigrant classification. You get an I-20 from the school

00:09:04.689 --> 00:09:07.439
that says yeah, you are accepted here, they are going to

00:09:07.439 --> 00:09:10.689
have you go to school, you take that I-20, you apply for an

00:09:10.689 --> 00:09:12.939
F-1 visa consulate. With the petition-based classification,

00:09:12.939 --> 00:09:16.100
we have to go through the immigration service in the United

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:19.629
States, get their approval on a piece of paper and then take

00:09:19.649 --> 00:09:24.679
that and get the visa. And that's important because the

00:09:24.699 --> 00:09:28.129
immigration service is not always the easiest government

00:09:28.149 --> 00:09:32.090
agency to deal with, and I am going to be careful what I say

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.610
because I know I have been videotaped but it's much

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:37.110
different when you are dealing with Stanford and they are

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.860
issuing I-20 and then you are going to the consular abroad

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:42.610
and they just say, "Oh, you are going to Stanford. That's

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:45.720
great. Here you go." So those are just some basic concepts.

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.639
The next thing that we are going to talk about are initial

00:09:48.639 --> 00:09:51.620
concerns for startups, and this is really geared to those of

00:09:51.639 --> 00:09:54.389
who you who are thinking about starting up a company when

00:09:54.389 --> 00:09:57.659
you graduate. You are on F-1 OPT status or J-1 status, and

00:09:57.659 --> 00:10:02.110
you want to start up a company here as one of the founders

00:10:02.129 --> 00:10:05.960
or one of the initial employees or maybe joining a startup.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.879
Maybe there is a startup that's already joined that has five

00:10:08.879 --> 00:10:11.379
or six employees, and you are thinking about joining that

00:10:11.379 --> 00:10:13.379
company as opposed to Google. These are important

00:10:13.379 --> 00:10:16.480
considerations. The USCIS, the immigration service, has a

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:21.389
fraud profile that targets smaller and newly-formed

00:10:21.409 --> 00:10:26.629
companies. And we have seen the fraud profile so it's not

00:10:26.649 --> 00:10:29.740
something, it's not a conspiracy theory. They accidentally

00:10:29.759 --> 00:10:33.319
leaked it. It's not on WikiLeaks yet but it was released to

00:10:33.339 --> 00:10:37.279
someone to a lawyer inadvertently. So we have all seen a

00:10:37.299 --> 00:10:41.750
copy of it. And these are some of the things that matter to

00:10:41.769 --> 00:10:43.590
the USCIS, size matters.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:46.519
Must have commercially zone premise. A commercial lease is

00:10:46.539 --> 00:10:49.269
best. So you want to start up a company at your apartment,

00:10:49.269 --> 00:10:53.399
and you want to get an H-1B, that's going to be a problem.

00:10:53.419 --> 00:10:57.189
The whole ides of Silicon Valley and garage startups doesn't

00:10:57.209 --> 00:11:01.730
work if you are on an F-1 and you are switching to an H-1B.

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:05.159
Must have money to pay salaries. There is prevailing wage

00:11:05.179 --> 00:11:08.929
requirements for the H-1B which we will talk about. And even

00:11:08.949 --> 00:11:11.029
for other nonimmigrant classifications, they want to see

00:11:11.049 --> 00:11:14.299
that you have got money in the bank that's going to pay the

00:11:14.299 --> 00:11:17.250
salary that's indicated on the H-1B petition. So sometimes

00:11:17.269 --> 00:11:20.059
people come and say, "We have got $50,000. We want to start

00:11:20.059 --> 00:11:23.309
a company, and my salary is going to be $100,000." It's hard

00:11:23.309 --> 00:11:26.809
to do that. They are going to ask where you are going to get

00:11:26.809 --> 00:11:29.059
the rest of the money to pay the salary.

00:11:29.059 --> 00:11:32.379
Must have working dedicated phone number. This is less of a

00:11:32.399 --> 00:11:35.000
concern. We haven't seen it as much but sometimes people,

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.980
they have their cell phone and they want us to put that on

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.000
the petition and the USCIS is going to call and they are

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.750
going to say, "Hey," I mean they want to hear someone's -

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:47.850
this is ACME, Inc. or whatever.

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.480
Must comply with all local state and federal ordinances,

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.819
obviously, and this sometimes causes confusion. You start up

00:11:53.839 --> 00:11:56.360
a company here in Palo Alto, it doesn't require business

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:59.399
license in Palo Alto but they are going to ask for that

00:11:59.419 --> 00:12:02.169
anyway. These are the kinds of things that they are looking

00:12:02.169 --> 00:12:05.740
for. If you have existing employees, they are going to want

00:12:05.759 --> 00:12:08.509
to see payroll records. So maybe you are going to say, "

00:12:08.509 --> 00:12:11.259
Okay, we have got three people working for us," maybe they

00:12:11.259 --> 00:12:14.009
are all working for equity. But if you don't have the

00:12:14.009 --> 00:12:16.509
payroll records, you can't really say that you have got

00:12:16.509 --> 00:12:19.509
three employees. They are going to want to see that they are

00:12:19.509 --> 00:12:24.220
regular paid employees with taxes withheld. And it's also

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.850
helpful to have insurance receipts for services, equipment

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:30.000
etc. Basically, what they are looking for is a lot of

00:12:30.019 --> 00:12:32.519
documentation to show that it's an actual business and sort

00:12:32.519 --> 00:12:38.230
of the view that the USCIS sees businesses. They don't see

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:41.000
them as startups working out of garages. They see them as

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:43.439
big organizations. Most of the petitions they see are from

00:12:43.439 --> 00:12:47.129
companies like IBM and Google. So they want to see all these

00:12:47.149 --> 00:12:48.149
little indicators.

00:12:48.149 --> 00:12:57.879
Audience

00:12:57.899 --> 00:13:02.269
So long as it's legal, it's fine. So yeah, VC funding is

00:13:02.289 --> 00:13:05.629
obviously is the way, I mean usually when we are doing a

00:13:05.649 --> 00:13:08.899
startup for an H-1B, there is no revenue. So we have to put

00:13:08.899 --> 00:13:12.289
on the form, we say $20 million hopefully in VC funding,

00:13:12.309 --> 00:13:14.629
usually somewhat less than that. But that's the idea.

00:13:14.629 --> 00:13:20.600
Audience

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:23.240
We can address that question afterwards. That's a little bit

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:28.419
more tricky and complicated. The newness of the company

00:13:28.439 --> 00:13:31.799
matters to USCIS as well. It's generally difficult for

00:13:31.819 --> 00:13:34.079
newly-formed companies to sponsor employees for petition-

00:13:34.099 --> 00:13:37.309
based work authorization because they don't have quarterly

00:13:37.329 --> 00:13:41.409
wage statements, again payroll records, tax returns, proven

00:13:41.429 --> 00:13:46.329
revenue, a track record of doing business, public records,

00:13:46.349 --> 00:13:51.250
and information on the web. One of the things that the USCIS

00:13:51.269 --> 00:13:53.769
has started doing recently is they have started using Dun &amp;

00:13:53.769 --> 00:13:56.949
Bradstreet which is a company that you might have heard of

00:13:56.969 --> 00:14:00.289
that tracks data about companies to verify the existence of

00:14:00.309 --> 00:14:03.779
companies, which is really a bad idea because Dun &amp;

00:14:03.799 --> 00:14:07.600
Bradstreet, frankly, is not always up to date. In fact, I

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:12.110
went to a stakeholders' meeting in DC talking about this new

00:14:12.129 --> 00:14:15.319
pilot program of using Dun &amp; Bradstreet data, and the Dun &amp;

00:14:15.339 --> 00:14:19.209
Bradstreet data people that were there said, "We have the

00:14:19.229 --> 00:14:21.979
data that's usually up to date." And I have my paralegal

00:14:21.979 --> 00:14:25.229
look up our data, and it was three or four years out of

00:14:25.229 --> 00:14:30.569
date. So I mean it's just another thing that indicates how

00:14:30.589 --> 00:14:32.850
difficult today is for smaller companies. The Dun &amp;

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:35.490
Bradstreet data for Google is probably pretty close up to

00:14:35.509 --> 00:14:37.969
date. It's not going to be for a startup company or they

00:14:37.969 --> 00:14:40.199
might not even be in Dun &amp; Bradstreet.

00:14:40.219 --> 00:14:44.069
So in terms of what needs to be done with this, if you are

00:14:44.089 --> 00:14:46.799
starting up a company, you are probably going to have to

00:14:46.799 --> 00:14:49.549
take all these steps that you wouldn't otherwise have to do.

00:14:49.549 --> 00:14:52.819
Go to Dun &amp; Bradstreet, get your company data in there,

00:14:52.839 --> 00:14:56.470
start paying employees rather than just have them on equity

00:14:56.490 --> 00:15:00.389
and get a commercial lease for office space.

00:15:00.409 --> 00:15:02.870
Stealth companies are problematic because there is no

00:15:02.889 --> 00:15:05.389
information on the Internet. A lot of times a startup

00:15:05.389 --> 00:15:08.139
company, as you know, it's going to in stealth mode. They

00:15:08.139 --> 00:15:11.389
don't want to tell people what they are doing. If we try to

00:15:11.389 --> 00:15:13.889
file an H-1B in that situation, it can be difficult,

00:15:13.889 --> 00:15:16.139
particularly if the company doesn't want to release anything

00:15:16.139 --> 00:15:18.589
to the immigration service. There is nothing on the website,

00:15:18.589 --> 00:15:22.149
nothing to verify what they are doing.

00:15:22.169 --> 00:15:25.199
So the way that we generally handle that is we have an

00:15:25.219 --> 00:15:27.719
explanation that this is a stealth company and we provide

00:15:27.719 --> 00:15:30.759
some documentation which we say to treat confidentially with

00:15:30.779 --> 00:15:33.069
the immigration service describing what the company is going

00:15:33.089 --> 00:15:34.089
to do.

00:15:34.089 --> 00:15:40.029
An owner employee immigration sponsorship, very difficult

00:15:40.049 --> 00:15:42.949
because the USCIS is new narrow interpretation of

00:15:42.969 --> 00:15:45.899
employment, which essentially states that sponsored

00:15:45.919 --> 00:15:49.129
employees must be controlled by others. So you want to start

00:15:49.149 --> 00:15:52.149
a company, you are going to be a co-founder. Let's say you

00:15:52.149 --> 00:15:55.399
are going to own 60% of the company. It's going to be very

00:15:55.399 --> 00:15:58.149
difficult to do an H-1B for that and other calcifications as

00:15:58.149 --> 00:16:01.459
well, but particularly H1B because they are going to say, "

00:16:01.479 --> 00:16:04.689
There is no employer-employee relationship because you, the

00:16:04.709 --> 00:16:08.100
employer, and you, the employee, are one in the same." So

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:10.359
that's the position that they take right now, and that makes

00:16:10.359 --> 00:16:15.199
it very difficult. And really, there is nothing you can do

00:16:15.219 --> 00:16:17.719
about that except try and do something where you don't have

00:16:17.719 --> 00:16:18.719
a majority ownership.

00:16:18.719 --> 00:16:19.719
Audience

00:16:19.719 --> 00:16:29.740
No. If you are the only founder, it depends, I mean there is

00:16:29.759 --> 00:16:31.929
other visa classifications. Right now, we are sort of

00:16:31.929 --> 00:16:34.569
talking about some of the H-1B, the petition-based visa

00:16:34.569 --> 00:16:38.990
processes but there might be other options that we will talk

00:16:39.009 --> 00:16:45.879
about here. But usually, for most people, H-1B is the only

00:16:45.899 --> 00:16:48.279
way that they have. But depending on your nationality and

00:16:48.279 --> 00:16:50.769
depending on how much money you are putting into it, there

00:16:50.769 --> 00:16:53.949
is other options.

00:16:53.969 --> 00:16:57.000
So now, we are going to talk about some of the common

00:16:57.019 --> 00:16:59.399
nonimmigrant visa classifications for people like you. First

00:16:59.419 --> 00:17:02.909
of all, one that you are very familiar with, F-1, as you

00:17:02.929 --> 00:17:05.710
know, when you graduate, you should get a year of optional

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:08.869
practical training. Some of you might be working pursuant to

00:17:08.889 --> 00:17:12.190
curricular practical training right now which allows you to

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:18.299
work while you are going to school. The F-1 OPT gives you 12

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:20.700
months once you graduate. Basically, you can work wherever

00:17:20.720 --> 00:17:24.139
you want so long as you are getting paid, and it's above

00:17:24.159 --> 00:17:26.690
minimum wage I guess.

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:30.950
So I mean that works actually very well for people that want

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:33.829
to start a company because you have that first year where

00:17:33.849 --> 00:17:36.609
really no one is looking over your shoulder. The USCIS is

00:17:36.629 --> 00:17:39.129
not saying, "Well, is that really work?" The only people

00:17:39.129 --> 00:17:41.879
that you have to deal with is the Stanford Foreign Studies

00:17:41.879 --> 00:17:44.129
Office, and as I understand, they are pretty understanding

00:17:44.129 --> 00:17:47.389
in this, and they are fine with students using their OPT to

00:17:47.409 --> 00:17:50.119
start a company.

00:17:50.139 --> 00:17:53.430
The next one is the H-1B Workers in Specialty Occupations,

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:56.849
and that's sort of the standard work visa, and we will talk

00:17:56.869 --> 00:18:00.980
more about that. But that's for anybody that's working in

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:03.629
the United States for an employer in what's called the

00:18:03.629 --> 00:18:07.490
Specialty Occupation, a position that requires a specific

00:18:07.509 --> 00:18:11.379
degree in a specific field. And so the quintessential H-1Bs

00:18:11.399 --> 00:18:17.639
are engineers, lawyers, those type of positions where it's

00:18:17.659 --> 00:18:21.309
easy to identify that you have to have a specific degree in

00:18:21.329 --> 00:18:23.460
a specific field.

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:26.769
The O-1, Alien of Extraordinary Ability, that's for people

00:18:26.789 --> 00:18:29.599
that have risen to the very top of their field. We often do

00:18:29.599 --> 00:18:32.670
that for people that are PhDs or startup founders that have

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:35.129
started other companies, raised lots of money, made lots of

00:18:35.129 --> 00:18:37.019
money, those types of things. It's very high standard to

00:18:37.019 --> 00:18:38.019
meet though.

00:18:38.019 --> 00:18:43.119
And the E-2, Treaty Investors, we will talk more about that

00:18:43.139 --> 00:18:46.139
as well. This is something that might work for some of you.

00:18:46.139 --> 00:18:50.649
Most countries we have treaties with that allow for

00:18:50.669 --> 00:18:54.509
investors - I shouldn't say most countries but most people

00:18:54.529 --> 00:18:57.420
are covered by treaties with countries that we have that

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:01.099
allow people from these countries to come here, start a

00:19:01.119 --> 00:19:05.750
business, start an investment, an enterprise and get a work

00:19:05.769 --> 00:19:09.529
visa to manage that business, to run that business. Now,

00:19:09.549 --> 00:19:12.119
there are some catches to that, and we will talk about that,

00:19:12.119 --> 00:19:17.500
but it is an option that should be looked at in all cases,

00:19:17.519 --> 00:19:19.710
and it's something that we have done for people that have

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.920
graduated from business school in the past year.

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:27.789
B-1, Business Visitor, that is a status that allows people

00:19:27.809 --> 00:19:30.230
to come here for purposes of business. It doesn't involve

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:34.799
work but you can use that to start a company or look into

00:19:34.819 --> 00:19:38.759
starting a company. But once the company is up and running,

00:19:38.779 --> 00:19:44.329
you can't really work in B-1 status.

00:19:44.349 --> 00:19:46.509
And then there is some other visa classifications, the TNs

00:19:46.509 --> 00:19:48.349
for Canada, I don't know...

00:19:48.369 --> 00:19:54.240
Audience

00:19:54.259 --> 00:19:56.750
Other visa classifications, the TNs which are available to

00:19:56.769 --> 00:20:00.740
Canadians and Mexicans, which is a work visa classification,

00:20:00.759 --> 00:20:03.809
the H1B1s which are available to Singaporeans and Chileans,

00:20:03.829 --> 00:20:08.500
I don't know if anyone is from Singapore or Chile here, and

00:20:08.519 --> 00:20:13.490
the E-3s which are available to Australian citizens. So we

00:20:13.509 --> 00:20:17.720
will just touch on those very briefly. So if you are not

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:20.849
from Canada, Mexico, Chile, Singapore, Australia, then you

00:20:20.869 --> 00:20:25.629
have a harder time.

00:20:25.649 --> 00:20:27.819
And thinking about the different options that are available,

00:20:27.819 --> 00:20:32.720
we have to ask which classification is best, and these are

00:20:32.740 --> 00:20:34.849
questions that I always ask clients with startups to

00:20:34.849 --> 00:20:38.839
determine that. When does employee's OPT, if applicable,

00:20:38.859 --> 00:20:43.079
expire? What country is the employee from? How far along if

00:20:43.099 --> 00:20:47.170
the company is setting up? How much capital has the startup

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:50.200
raised and from what source? "From what source" is very

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:52.679
important so is the amount but "from what source" is very

00:20:52.679 --> 00:20:55.819
important. If it comes from a VC in the United States, it

00:20:55.839 --> 00:20:59.779
will preclude out possibly other options for investor

00:20:59.799 --> 00:21:05.609
research. How much ownership interest will the employee have

00:21:05.629 --> 00:21:08.079
in the company? And as I mentioned, that's a relevant

00:21:08.079 --> 00:21:11.960
concern because it can be difficult to get a work visa where

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:15.279
the employee that's being sponsored is also a majority

00:21:15.299 --> 00:21:19.480
owner. On the other hand, it will open up other options,

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:23.589
potentially for some people. What is the nature of the

00:21:23.609 --> 00:21:26.539
business and how big will the operation be? So I just

00:21:26.559 --> 00:21:29.629
mentioned this as sort of an introduction. As we go in more

00:21:29.649 --> 00:21:33.200
detail to the different visa classifications, you will

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:39.750
understand why these questions are relevant.

00:21:39.769 --> 00:21:43.619
F-1 practical training, as I mentioned, is issues for

00:21:43.639 --> 00:21:46.909
startups, requires employment so must get paid. So you can't

00:21:46.929 --> 00:21:49.940
just hang out on your OPT, unfortunately. You have got to

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:53.289
get money from somewhere. And as I understand most schools

00:21:53.309 --> 00:21:56.059
and I think Stanford as well, they are pretty flexible with

00:21:56.059 --> 00:21:59.549
that, you don't have to get paid a whole bunch of money but

00:21:59.549 --> 00:22:04.259
you do need to get paid somehow. There is two types of

00:22:04.279 --> 00:22:06.579
practical training, again the optional practical training,

00:22:06.599 --> 00:22:09.769
generally granted for up to 12 months. It's usually post

00:22:09.789 --> 00:22:13.750
completion. You can get OPT prior to graduating but usually

00:22:13.769 --> 00:22:16.149
people wait, they save it till like they graduate and they

00:22:16.149 --> 00:22:19.529
take their full 12 months then.

00:22:19.549 --> 00:22:21.889
Must notify the school of the employer, that's very

00:22:21.889 --> 00:22:24.889
important, that's sort of a new rule a couple of years ago.

00:22:24.889 --> 00:22:27.889
So when you start working somewhere, you have got to go back

00:22:27.889 --> 00:22:30.889
to Stanford and say, "Hey, I am working here" and let them

00:22:30.889 --> 00:22:33.389
know. You can't have anymore than 90 days of unemployment.

00:22:33.389 --> 00:22:36.259
So you can not work for a little bit of time but if you

00:22:36.259 --> 00:22:40.549
exceed that 90 days, then you are in violation of status.

00:22:40.569 --> 00:22:44.220
And also important is it has to be related to a degree

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:47.789
program. So if you are using your OPT to work as a waiter,

00:22:47.809 --> 00:22:51.059
it might be difficult case to make but it's related to your

00:22:51.079 --> 00:22:54.149
MBA program.

00:22:54.169 --> 00:22:56.849
Curricular practical training is essentially an internship

00:22:56.869 --> 00:23:00.440
while you are going to school. So people get curricular

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:06.430
practical training to work at companies like IBM or Google

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:09.809
or whatever. You can also use it with a startup but it must

00:23:09.829 --> 00:23:12.399
be part of the curriculum, and it has to be specifically

00:23:12.399 --> 00:23:17.250
approved by the Foreign Studies Office here. So they need to

00:23:17.269 --> 00:23:20.250
look at what you are doing and I am sure they have some

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:23.500
forms that you need to fill out, and they need to approve it

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:24.750
before you can do it.

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:25.000
Audience

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:38.399
90 days total.

00:23:38.419 --> 00:24:00.730
Audience

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:05.889
I don't think there is anything new but there are some

00:24:05.909 --> 00:24:09.139
specific things about self-employment in the regulations but

00:24:09.159 --> 00:24:16.210
we can talk about that. But yeah, OPT, you can use to - I

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:20.710
mean self-employment is you can work as an independent

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:24.119
contractor, for example, on OPT. You get like a 1099 but you

00:24:24.139 --> 00:24:27.730
do have to be receiving compensation. So I guess it depends

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:33.079
on what you mean by self-employment if you are just building

00:24:33.099 --> 00:24:35.599
a house and not getting paid for it, that would...

00:24:34.029 --> 00:24:35.029
Audience

00:24:35.029 --> 00:24:50.399
Yeah, that's fine. You can do it but again, you can't just

00:24:50.419 --> 00:24:52.779
be self-employed and not making money because that's

00:24:52.779 --> 00:24:56.809
essentially not doing anything, or just doing a hobby.

00:24:56.829 --> 00:25:05.379
Audience

00:25:05.399 --> 00:25:09.009
The way it work is if you use a full year of CPT, then you

00:25:09.009 --> 00:25:11.740
are ineligible for OPT. But conceivably, you can do like six

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:14.329
months of CPT and then still have your one year of OPT. Does

00:25:14.329 --> 00:25:15.329
that make sense.

00:25:15.329 --> 00:25:17.740
Audience

00:25:17.759 --> 00:25:20.399
Okay. Let's talk about the H-1B, Workers in Specialty

00:25:20.399 --> 00:25:23.759
Occupations. Again, this is the most common work visa. It's

00:25:23.779 --> 00:25:27.359
for workers in positions that normally require at least a

00:25:27.379 --> 00:25:31.429
Bachelors degree in a specific field, and again engineers

00:25:31.449 --> 00:25:34.279
and lawyers are sort of quintessential ones. It does not

00:25:34.299 --> 00:25:36.210
require

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:39.519
[inaudible], as I mentioned. There is a six-year maximum

00:25:39.539 --> 00:25:47.079
time limit. So people in H1B status can only get six years

00:25:47.099 --> 00:25:53.079
total. However, if you start the Green Card process within

00:25:53.099 --> 00:25:57.089
enough time, usually that's before the end of your fifth

00:25:57.109 --> 00:26:02.839
year as an H-1B, then you can extend your H-1B indefinitely

00:26:02.859 --> 00:26:05.829
while that Green Card process is going. And that's very

00:26:05.849 --> 00:26:08.049
important for some people from certain countries because

00:26:08.069 --> 00:26:10.939
there is huge backlogs for Green Cards. So if you are from

00:26:10.939 --> 00:26:13.339
India or China, the backlog could be as long as eight or

00:26:13.339 --> 00:26:16.809
nine years. And if you only get six years as an H-1B, then

00:26:16.809 --> 00:26:19.559
obviously that's going to be a problem. So they changed the

00:26:19.559 --> 00:26:22.309
law in 2000 that now allows you to extend that H-1B

00:26:22.309 --> 00:26:26.059
indefinitely while you are waiting for your Green Card so

00:26:26.079 --> 00:26:29.579
long as you start the Green Card process soon enough. And

00:26:29.599 --> 00:26:32.339
that's something that's unique to the H-1B.

00:26:32.359 --> 00:26:35.349
Another important feature to the H-1B is that you can port

00:26:35.349 --> 00:26:38.480
from one employer to another before the petitions approve.

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:41.460
So you work at one company, you want to work at another

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:45.009
company, once the new company files the H-1B petition, you

00:26:45.029 --> 00:26:48.950
can start working for the new company. That's unique to the

00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:59.409
H-1B as well.

00:26:59.429 --> 00:27:00.429
[Audience]

00:27:00.429 --> 00:27:04.480
No, it doesn't. Unfortunately, question was in case whether

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:11.829
the H-1B six-year limit is reset by going to F-1 status. It

00:27:11.849 --> 00:27:15.169
is not. While you are in F-1 status, you don't use that six

00:27:15.169 --> 00:27:18.599
years so you still have the balance of what you had before.

00:27:18.619 --> 00:27:21.450
However, the only way that you can reset it is being

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:25.829
physically out of the United States for one year.

00:27:25.849 --> 00:27:26.849
[Audience]

00:27:26.849 --> 00:27:28.970
Continuous year, yeah, you still have the balance.

00:27:28.990 --> 00:27:29.990
[Audience]

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:59.990
Yeah. Every time you file a new H-1B petition with a new

00:28:11.059 --> 00:28:13.809
employer, it's all the same fees, all the same paperwork.

00:28:13.829 --> 00:28:17.549
There are some advantages like the portability, so you can

00:28:17.569 --> 00:28:20.220
port from one employer to other. You don't have to wait for

00:28:20.220 --> 00:28:22.970
it to be approved but it's the same paperwork. So sometimes

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:25.699
people say, "It's just an H-1B transfer." There is no real

00:28:25.699 --> 00:28:27.799
such thing as an "H-1B transfer," it's always a new

00:28:27.799 --> 00:28:30.039
petition.

00:28:30.059 --> 00:28:31.059
[Audience]

00:28:31.059 --> 00:28:33.250
The government fees?

00:28:33.269 --> 00:28:34.269
[Audience]

00:28:34.269 --> 00:28:38.450
Well, it depends on the attorney's fees, and well it also

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:40.689
depends on the size of the company and whether you want

00:28:40.689 --> 00:28:43.079
premium processing and there is a lot of things. But

00:28:43.079 --> 00:28:46.079
generally it's going to be for most $4,000 to $5,000.

00:28:46.079 --> 00:28:47.079
[Audience]

00:28:47.079 --> 00:29:05.480
Well, that's why with a startup, depending on the position

00:29:05.500 --> 00:29:09.429
and depending on how big the company is, your immigration

00:29:09.449 --> 00:29:12.819
attorney will hopefully tell you that let's wait till it's

00:29:12.839 --> 00:29:16.720
approved, rather than having quit, because if it does get

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:22.480
denied, then he is in a hard situation. So I always tell -

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:25.730
if I think it's clearly going to be approved, then I will

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:28.730
say, "Yeah, go ahead and have them change." But if it's one

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:31.980
of those cases that I think the USCIS might give us a hard

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:34.730
time on it, we file premium processing. It's only going to

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:38.710
take a couple weeks anyway. So let's just wait till then.

00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:42.970
So challenges for startups and entrepreneurs in the H-1B,

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:45.740
again, the fraud profile which is specifically for the H-1B.

00:29:45.759 --> 00:29:50.539
They are looking at the size of the company. And when the

00:29:50.559 --> 00:29:55.480
USCIS is adjudicating small company H-1Bs, they interpret

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:59.019
specialty occupation much more narrowly than they do for

00:29:59.039 --> 00:30:01.970
large companies, and there are certain kinds of positions,

00:30:01.990 --> 00:30:04.809
and unfortunately business positions, which they are going

00:30:04.829 --> 00:30:08.439
to scrutinize very, very closely. So if you have a startup

00:30:08.459 --> 00:30:11.220
company with five or six people, and let's say it's got $1

00:30:11.220 --> 00:30:13.970
million in VC funding and you are hiring a software engineer

00:30:13.970 --> 00:30:16.970
with a degree in computer science, does it work for you? You

00:30:16.970 --> 00:30:19.970
are probably not going to have a problem with that. But if

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:22.960
you are hiring a business systems analyst or a director of

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:26.769
marketing, much more likely that you are going to have a

00:30:26.789 --> 00:30:29.789
problem with that. It's not that you can't do it and the

00:30:29.789 --> 00:30:32.289
immigration service has got a little bit better over the

00:30:32.289 --> 00:30:35.289
last year, but it is a more problematic case. So that's one

00:30:35.289 --> 00:30:38.289
of those cases, if it were a portability case, you might say

00:30:38.289 --> 00:30:41.629
let's just wait for it to be approved just to play it safe.

00:30:41.629 --> 00:30:42.629
[Audience]

00:30:42.629 --> 00:30:48.509
Well, they look at both but usually degree is not a problem.

00:30:48.529 --> 00:30:54.500
I mean someone has a degree in engineering and they have an

00:30:54.519 --> 00:30:57.240
MBA, they are not going to say, "Well, is that really an

00:30:57.240 --> 00:31:00.220
MBA." I heard Stanford is a good school but they are really

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:03.629
looking at it. And the things that they look at is like you

00:31:03.649 --> 00:31:06.399
have got five people, why do you need a director of

00:31:06.399 --> 00:31:09.990
marketing. So they sort of impose their business judgment in

00:31:10.009 --> 00:31:20.179
the process of adjudicating your H-1B.

00:31:20.199 --> 00:31:21.199
[Audience]

00:31:21.199 --> 00:31:24.849
That's a complicated question. There is a certain portion of

00:31:24.869 --> 00:31:27.309
the government filing fees that the employee cannot pay by

00:31:27.309 --> 00:31:31.559
law. However, there are reasons that the employee should not

00:31:31.579 --> 00:31:34.669
pay other portions of it depending on how much the person is

00:31:34.689 --> 00:31:37.799
making relative to other employees of the company. And I

00:31:37.819 --> 00:31:40.159
know that's not a very clear answer but just in the interest

00:31:40.159 --> 00:31:42.359
of time, we can take that up in the after session if you

00:31:42.359 --> 00:31:43.359
like.

00:31:43.359 --> 00:31:44.359
[Audience]

00:31:44.359 --> 00:31:56.669
Yeah, there is an annual cap for H-1Bs, and I think we get

00:31:56.689 --> 00:32:00.689
to that next slide. So I will just wait, and we'll talk

00:32:00.709 --> 00:32:11.349
about that.

00:32:11.369 --> 00:32:12.369
[Audience]

00:32:12.369 --> 00:32:18.429
Absolutely. Well, I mean one tactic or strategy, I am not

00:32:18.449 --> 00:32:23.349
sure which, is to try to file an H-1B if it's - you get to

00:32:23.369 --> 00:32:26.389
give yourself multiple bites at the apple. So sometimes,

00:32:26.409 --> 00:32:29.359
people do that. The company is kind of small now, why don't

00:32:29.359 --> 00:32:32.609
we try it now. I mean it costs money obviously, and you are

00:32:32.609 --> 00:32:35.859
going to lose all that money if it gets denied. And then if

00:32:35.859 --> 00:32:38.859
it doesn't work out, you still have your OPT. I mean there

00:32:38.859 --> 00:32:41.359
are some issues with that, there are some logistical issues

00:32:41.359 --> 00:32:44.210
with that. But sometimes, that makes sense because one of

00:32:44.230 --> 00:32:47.480
the things, and we will talk about this with the cap, is if

00:32:47.480 --> 00:32:50.460
the economy starts really moving again, it might be very

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:53.649
difficult to get an H-1B. Now, I think everybody here is

00:32:53.669 --> 00:32:56.379
graduating with MBA so you get a special set-aside, your

00:32:56.379 --> 00:32:59.779
chances of getting one are better. Again, I am sort of

00:32:59.799 --> 00:33:04.159
jumping the gun and talking about the cap. But what happened

00:33:04.179 --> 00:33:09.099
in 2007 is that even people with masters, even 90% of them

00:33:09.119 --> 00:33:12.919
got H-1B. You had to file on one day. So if you were an

00:33:12.939 --> 00:33:17.529
unlucky one in 10, your case was unlucky and you didn't get

00:33:17.549 --> 00:33:20.259
picked in the lottery. And so sometimes, it makes sense to

00:33:20.259 --> 00:33:23.189
say, "Okay, I've got the year of OPT but let's try filing

00:33:23.189 --> 00:33:26.189
now just in case the economy is going to get really, really

00:33:26.189 --> 00:33:29.689
good next year and I am not going to have a chance in the

00:33:29.689 --> 00:33:32.939
lottery." Again, I am sorry, I am sort of jumping the gun on

00:33:32.939 --> 00:33:33.939
talking about the cap.

00:33:33.939 --> 00:33:36.439
So the H-1B is problematic, as I mentioned, for executives

00:33:36.439 --> 00:33:38.439
and managers because they are not traditionally specialty

00:33:38.439 --> 00:33:41.730
occupations. Again, it doesn't mean that you can't make it

00:33:41.750 --> 00:33:44.609
happen but it's more difficult and the risk of a denial is

00:33:44.609 --> 00:33:48.220
higher. What we tend to do is if we are doing someone with

00:33:48.240 --> 00:33:50.879
the masters degree, and MBA, and it's sort of a technical

00:33:50.879 --> 00:33:53.859
position and they have an undergraduate technical degree, we

00:33:53.879 --> 00:33:56.879
will sort of pin the position on that because that's more of

00:33:56.879 --> 00:33:59.129
a specialty occupation.

00:33:59.149 --> 00:34:00.149
[Audience]

00:34:00.149 --> 00:34:15.199
I mean if you get promoted to CEO, then we have to file an

00:34:15.219 --> 00:34:18.469
amendment if you are H-1B, is normally what we do. And if we

00:34:18.469 --> 00:34:21.969
were to do that CEO, we would still try and pin it on your

00:34:21.969 --> 00:34:24.469
technical background. So this isn't just the CEO, like the

00:34:24.469 --> 00:34:26.539
CEO of U-Haul. It requires someone with a technical

00:34:26.539 --> 00:34:35.869
background. I don't know why U-Haul came to my mind but...

00:34:35.889 --> 00:34:38.139
And again, as we mentioned, it's increasingly difficult

00:34:38.159 --> 00:34:40.619
whether the sponsored H-1B employee has ownership interest

00:34:40.639 --> 00:34:45.019
in the company. If you have 25%-30% and we have all the

00:34:45.039 --> 00:34:47.460
industries of a employment relationship and employment

00:34:47.480 --> 00:34:51.469
agreement, offer letter, all that stuff, pay stubs, it's not

00:34:51.489 --> 00:34:54.750
likely going to be a problem. But if you own 50% or more,

00:34:54.769 --> 00:34:57.340
then it could be a problem.

00:34:57.360 --> 00:35:02.110
And finally, downsides of the H-1B, it's relatively

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:04.309
expensive in terms government filling fees. As I mentioned,

00:35:04.309 --> 00:35:06.699
it's usually between $4,000 and $5,000 with premium

00:35:06.699 --> 00:35:11.960
processing, depending on the size of the company. More

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:16.559
downsides, annual cap, annual cap is 65,000, and of these,

00:35:16.579 --> 00:35:19.900
6,700 are reserved for Chileans and Singaporeans. Remember I

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:22.779
said they have a special treaty status for Singapore and

00:35:22.799 --> 00:35:25.759
Chile. Well, they took those visa numbers in their treaty

00:35:25.779 --> 00:35:30.460
out of the general pot and reserved it for them. And being

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:34.559
that Singapore is a small country and there is not a lot of

00:35:34.579 --> 00:35:36.880
people coming from Chile coming here, they don't use that

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:40.710
whole 6,700. So what happens is this that that unused

00:35:40.730 --> 00:35:44.049
portion gets put into account for the next year, and that's

00:35:44.069 --> 00:35:47.319
where we are at right now this year. So last year, I think

00:35:47.319 --> 00:35:51.670
there was only a few hundred H1-B1 for Chile and Singapore,

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:56.779
and they just added those to the - or indicated how many of

00:35:56.799 --> 00:35:59.519
those were left over for this year. So we have actually

00:35:59.519 --> 00:36:04.549
reached the normal path for fiscal year 2011 which is 65,000

00:36:04.569 --> 00:36:08.980
minus 6,700. But because most of that 6,700 is left over

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:11.519
from last year gets added to the pot, so we still have about

00:36:11.519 --> 00:36:13.769
3,000 or 4,000 left for this year.

00:36:13.769 --> 00:36:14.769
[Audience]

00:36:14.769 --> 00:36:18.179
No, fiscal year starts October 1st. Fiscal year 2011 started

00:36:18.199 --> 00:36:26.509
on October 1st, 2010. So if you look at the numbers that

00:36:26.529 --> 00:36:29.759
were released a couple of weeks ago, and it shows that the

00:36:29.779 --> 00:36:34.829
61,000 regular H-1Bs that have been used up, oh no, the caps

00:36:34.849 --> 00:36:37.089
have been reached, but it's not because we have these

00:36:37.089 --> 00:36:39.730
leftovers from Chile and Singapore last year.

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:42.489
Now, for all of you that are graduating with an MBA, there

00:36:42.489 --> 00:36:46.819
is 20,000 in addition to that that are set aside for people

00:36:46.839 --> 00:36:50.809
with Masters degree from U.S. schools. That cap for this

00:36:50.829 --> 00:36:54.369
fiscal year has already been reached. That doesn't mean you

00:36:54.389 --> 00:36:56.889
can't get an H-1B because we haven't reached the regular

00:36:56.889 --> 00:37:02.529
cap, it's just that special set-aside has been reached. And

00:37:02.549 --> 00:37:05.559
what happens when there is a huge demand for H-1B, as like

00:37:05.559 --> 00:37:11.110
there was in 2007, the regular H-1Bs would run out first. So

00:37:11.130 --> 00:37:13.710
it really meant something to have those additional 20,000

00:37:13.730 --> 00:37:17.799
for Masters degree holders. But what we are seeing is there

00:37:17.819 --> 00:37:20.569
is a lot more people graduating with Masters. So it's quite

00:37:20.569 --> 00:37:24.219
possible that the Masters cap and the regular H-1B cap, when

00:37:24.239 --> 00:37:30.069
the economy gets good, will run out around the same time.

00:37:30.089 --> 00:37:31.089
[Audience]

00:37:31.089 --> 00:37:34.889
It depends. If you held H-1B status in the last six years,

00:37:34.909 --> 00:37:37.909
and you still have time left, you haven't used up your whole

00:37:37.909 --> 00:37:40.409
six years, yeah, that's correct. The question was if you

00:37:40.409 --> 00:37:43.639
have had an H-1B in the past, you went back to school to get

00:37:43.639 --> 00:37:46.940
an MBA, can you reactivate that H-1B without being subject

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:49.059
to the cap, and the answer is yes, if it meets those

00:37:49.059 --> 00:37:50.059
conditions.

00:37:50.059 --> 00:37:51.059
[Audience]

00:37:51.059 --> 00:38:07.610
No, it doesn't. The way that cap works is if you have been

00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:11.799
counted against the cap once in the last six years, you are

00:38:11.819 --> 00:38:15.659
not subject to the cap. That's just a gloss but it's a

00:38:15.679 --> 00:38:18.199
little bit more complicated than that. So essentially, what

00:38:18.219 --> 00:38:21.299
I tell people if you have held H-1B status in the past,

00:38:21.299 --> 00:38:26.219
chances are you are not subject to the annual

00:38:26.239 --> 00:38:27.239
[inaudible].

00:38:27.239 --> 00:38:30.250
Another downside of the H-1B, there is a lot of employer

00:38:30.269 --> 00:38:32.269
restrictions on it. Unlike most other visa classifications,

00:38:32.289 --> 00:38:35.400
the employer has to pay a prevailing wage, and they have to

00:38:35.420 --> 00:38:40.449
keep very detailed records on payroll and working conditions

00:38:40.469 --> 00:38:43.130
in compliance with the Department of Labor Regulations. So

00:38:43.150 --> 00:38:47.339
for startups, that's particularly difficult because you

00:38:47.359 --> 00:38:50.079
probably have not very much administrative support.

00:38:50.099 --> 00:38:52.489
Everybody is wearing multiple hats, and then you have to

00:38:52.489 --> 00:38:55.369
read this long instruction book that we send people that

00:38:55.389 --> 00:38:58.599
explains how to do all the stuff. Obviously, for bigger

00:38:58.619 --> 00:39:01.279
company, they have a whole department and probably a person

00:39:01.299 --> 00:39:05.589
specifically doing that, and that's all they do.

00:39:05.609 --> 00:39:09.489
But that is a downside, and part of the reasons that that's

00:39:09.509 --> 00:39:12.029
difficult for startups is that when you start a company,

00:39:12.049 --> 00:39:16.000
let's say you are going to be the CEO and you want to take a

00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:19.389
small salary and just take equity for most of your

00:39:19.409 --> 00:39:22.449
compensation, you can't do that with the H-1B. There is a

00:39:22.449 --> 00:39:25.449
prevailing wage requirement. You have to be paid what the

00:39:25.469 --> 00:39:30.230
Department of Labor says CEO should be making in the area of

00:39:30.250 --> 00:39:34.029
intended employment, and that figure is very high. So when

00:39:34.049 --> 00:39:37.860
doing an H-1B for a startup, there has to be some sort of

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:40.880
flexibility in what your role is going to be at the company.

00:39:40.880 --> 00:39:48.250
If you file an H-1B for a CEO, $150,000, and they are not

00:39:48.269 --> 00:39:51.019
going to consider equity or bonuses. It has to be salary

00:39:51.019 --> 00:39:53.029
paid at least once a month.

00:39:53.049 --> 00:39:56.559
Finally, no spousal work authorization for H-1B. Some other

00:39:56.579 --> 00:40:02.170
visa classification that we will talk about, your spouse can

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:06.309
work in basically open market employment while you are here.

00:40:06.329 --> 00:40:08.159
It doesn't apply to H1B.

00:40:08.179 --> 00:40:09.179
[Informal Talk]

00:40:09.179 --> 00:40:19.289
O-1, Aliens of Extraordinary Ability, again, this is for

00:40:19.309 --> 00:40:21.409
people that are really at the top of their fields. So

00:40:21.409 --> 00:40:25.789
someone who has documented achievements in business startups

00:40:25.809 --> 00:40:30.000
or technology, we often do O-1s for them. People with PhDs,

00:40:30.019 --> 00:40:34.569
it's usually a pretty good choice. Usually, if the H-1B is

00:40:34.589 --> 00:40:37.319
available as an option, we will start there because even

00:40:37.339 --> 00:40:40.589
though the filing fees are less for the O-1, it's a lot of

00:40:40.589 --> 00:40:43.610
preparation. It's a lot of work, and the person really has

00:40:43.630 --> 00:40:48.569
to be able to show a lot of documents demonstrating their

00:40:48.589 --> 00:40:54.360
extraordinary abilities. So I am just going to sort of skip

00:40:54.380 --> 00:40:57.929
through that because I think that's less important than some

00:40:57.949 --> 00:40:58.949
of these other things.

00:40:58.949 --> 00:41:01.710
The next thing I want to talk about is the E-2, Treaty

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:04.190
Investor, and this applies to people from a lot of

00:41:04.190 --> 00:41:09.110
countries. We have treaties with countries, many countries,

00:41:09.130 --> 00:41:14.599
most countries in Western Europe, many countries in Asia,

00:41:14.619 --> 00:41:19.049
which allow for people to come to the United States to start

00:41:19.069 --> 00:41:23.409
a business and get a visa pursuant to that business and

00:41:23.429 --> 00:41:25.719
running that business. But there are some important

00:41:25.739 --> 00:41:28.599
restrictions that are relevant to people like you who are

00:41:28.619 --> 00:41:31.369
going to be starting up a company and possibly seeking VC

00:41:31.369 --> 00:41:32.630
funding.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:36.219
First of all, the enterprise has to be at least 50% owned

00:41:36.239 --> 00:41:39.259
and controlled by nationals of the treaty country. So if you

00:41:39.279 --> 00:41:42.429
are coming here, and you are going to start a company, and

00:41:42.449 --> 00:41:46.199
you are not going to own at least 50% of it or you are not

00:41:46.199 --> 00:41:48.949
going to have other people from your country owning 50% of

00:41:48.949 --> 00:41:51.900
it, then the E-2 is not going to work. And that's often the

00:41:51.900 --> 00:41:55.009
case because you might be co-founding with some U.S. people

00:41:55.029 --> 00:41:57.829
or you might be receiving so much VC funding but you are not

00:41:57.829 --> 00:42:01.440
going to have at least 50% ownership. So that's an important

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:05.829
restriction. It's available to people at managerial,

00:42:05.849 --> 00:42:09.719
executive, or specialized positions. So if you are running a

00:42:09.739 --> 00:42:12.279
business, as an executive or manager, you can qualify. If

00:42:12.299 --> 00:42:15.049
you are an engineer or sort of a highly specialized position

00:42:15.049 --> 00:42:19.150
at the company, you can also qualify. So it's not only the

00:42:19.170 --> 00:42:22.670
investors that can use the E-2. Let's say you start a

00:42:22.690 --> 00:42:27.349
company, and you do your own E-2 to run a company, and then

00:42:27.369 --> 00:42:33.269
you want to hire someone from, let say it's Germany, and you

00:42:33.289 --> 00:42:36.539
decide you want to hire some guy that you know is a great

00:42:36.539 --> 00:42:39.789
engineer, he is not going own any of the company. He is not

00:42:39.789 --> 00:42:43.039
going to manage it but he is a great engineer. You can hire

00:42:43.039 --> 00:42:45.539
that person pursuant to the E-2 classification, and it's a

00:42:45.539 --> 00:42:48.039
lot less - it's going to ultimately be less expensive and

00:42:48.039 --> 00:42:49.610
less restrictive than the H-1B.

00:42:49.630 --> 00:42:52.159
It's also based on a substantial investment in the United

00:42:52.159 --> 00:42:55.409
States. So let's say you come here, you are starting a

00:42:55.429 --> 00:42:59.079
company, you are going own 50% or maybe more but all the

00:42:59.099 --> 00:43:03.589
cash is coming from a VC in the United States, that's going

00:43:03.609 --> 00:43:06.049
to be a problem. You have to show that there is a

00:43:06.049 --> 00:43:09.619
substantial amount of money coming from the investors of the

00:43:09.639 --> 00:43:13.179
treaty country. And it doesn't necessarily have to be money.

00:43:13.199 --> 00:43:15.449
It can be equipment. Theoretically, it can be intellectual

00:43:15.449 --> 00:43:18.170
property but that's very, very difficult because you have to

00:43:18.170 --> 00:43:22.619
provide an objective valuation of it.

00:43:22.639 --> 00:43:25.829
Some of the benefits, it allows spouse work authorization.

00:43:25.849 --> 00:43:29.440
So if you are coming on an E-2, your husband or wife can

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:33.380
work anywhere he or she wants while you are on the E-2. Very

00:43:33.400 --> 00:43:37.139
few employer restrictions, it does not require - and this is

00:43:37.159 --> 00:43:40.730
probably one of the best benefits - it does not require a

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:42.719
petition with the USCIS ahead of time.

00:43:42.739 --> 00:43:45.949
So again, one of the issues with the H-1B and other

00:43:45.969 --> 00:43:49.809
petition-based work authorized classifications is we have to

00:43:49.829 --> 00:43:52.579
deal with the immigration service, and they tend to be a

00:43:52.579 --> 00:43:56.420
little less open minded frankly. The counselor service is

00:43:56.440 --> 00:44:01.659
what adjudicates most E-2 applications, and their training

00:44:01.679 --> 00:44:08.029
is more to facilitate international trade and help realize

00:44:08.049 --> 00:44:11.519
the goal of these treaties, which is to allow people from

00:44:11.539 --> 00:44:14.279
the U.S. to go to that country and people from that country

00:44:14.279 --> 00:44:22.569
to come here to invest. So the E-2 I think is much more -

00:44:22.589 --> 00:44:28.139
the adjudication of the E-2 is much more reasonable.

00:44:28.159 --> 00:44:31.289
Again, the downside, substantial investment in the U.S., so

00:44:31.309 --> 00:44:34.980
representative office or sales office, don't qualify.

00:44:35.000 --> 00:44:36.839
Processing generally takes much longer than other

00:44:36.859 --> 00:44:40.019
classifications depending on the country. In Canada, they

00:44:40.039 --> 00:44:44.359
can take several months. In Chile, it might only take a

00:44:44.379 --> 00:44:47.549
couple of weeks.

00:44:47.569 --> 00:44:48.569
[Audience]

00:44:48.569 --> 00:45:01.900
No. I mean there is a lot of documentation that you have to

00:45:01.920 --> 00:45:04.879
show with the E-2, much more so than even the H-1B.

00:45:04.879 --> 00:45:05.879
[Audience]

00:45:05.879 --> 00:45:08.400
It just means that you don't have to file a petition with

00:45:08.400 --> 00:45:10.599
the immigration service prior to getting classification. You

00:45:10.619 --> 00:45:18.690
apply directly at the consulate or embassy abroad.

00:45:18.710 --> 00:45:19.710
[Audience]

00:45:19.710 --> 00:45:22.279
Substantial investment depends on the nature of the

00:45:22.299 --> 00:45:25.500
enterprise. So if you are starting a hotdog stand, the

00:45:25.519 --> 00:45:28.500
substantial investment is going to be a lot less than a

00:45:28.519 --> 00:45:30.119
factory.

00:45:30.139 --> 00:45:31.139
[Audience]

00:45:31.139 --> 00:45:35.599
My role with them, the absolute minimum is $100,000. There

00:45:35.619 --> 00:45:39.000
is nothing in the regulations that says but we won't do an

00:45:39.019 --> 00:45:43.869
E-2 unless there is $100,000. If someone says that they are

00:45:43.889 --> 00:45:46.699
going to open a semiconductor fab, and they say their

00:45:46.719 --> 00:45:49.119
investments are $100,000, obviously that's not going to

00:45:49.119 --> 00:45:51.389
work. It just really depends on the enterprise. And you

00:45:51.389 --> 00:45:54.190
know, when doing an E-2, we need a business plan that all

00:45:54.190 --> 00:45:57.440
has to make sense in terms of the business plan. If you have

00:45:57.440 --> 00:46:00.190
this amount of investment, is that going to work for what

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:03.690
you are planning on doing. So I am going to just sort of try

00:46:03.690 --> 00:46:06.190
and go through this. The B-1, visitor status, again, does

00:46:06.190 --> 00:46:09.190
not allow work authorization but it is a status that you can

00:46:09.190 --> 00:46:11.690
use to explore starting a business and actually start a

00:46:11.690 --> 00:46:14.690
business. So you can come on a B-1 business visitor, or the

00:46:14.690 --> 00:46:17.690
visa waiver if you qualify if you are from a visa waiver

00:46:17.690 --> 00:46:20.190
country, and use that, talk to potential investors, look for

00:46:20.190 --> 00:46:24.409
office space, even hire people. But once the business is up

00:46:24.429 --> 00:46:29.579
and running, you need to switch to L-1 or E-2 status if the

00:46:29.599 --> 00:46:32.460
company is qualified.

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:33.480
[Audience]

00:46:33.480 --> 00:46:37.769
Yeah. I mean basically, when you are actually start

00:46:37.789 --> 00:46:41.619
receiving money from clients, or I would say even before

00:46:41.639 --> 00:46:44.679
that, getting paid if you are - definitely, if you are

00:46:44.699 --> 00:46:47.699
getting paid by the company, or you definitely need to be in

00:46:47.699 --> 00:46:50.199
work authorized status. But even if you are not getting

00:46:50.199 --> 00:46:52.699
paid, and the company is starting to generate revenue, you

00:46:52.699 --> 00:46:55.199
really should be in a work authorized status. There is

00:46:55.199 --> 00:46:57.699
nothing that really delineates that in the statute of the

00:46:57.699 --> 00:46:59.619
regulations but...

00:46:59.639 --> 00:47:00.639
[Audience]

00:47:00.639 --> 00:47:04.809
The B-1? It depends on - if you come in on a visa waiver, so

00:47:04.829 --> 00:47:08.079
if you are from a visa waiver country, it's 90 days at a

00:47:08.079 --> 00:47:11.549
time. Then you have to leave, and then you can come back. If

00:47:11.569 --> 00:47:14.849
you actually apply for B-1 Visa, it will only be granted for

00:47:14.869 --> 00:47:17.799
up to six months at a time. And often times, they will grant

00:47:17.799 --> 00:47:20.799
it for much less depending on what your purposes are. So if

00:47:20.799 --> 00:47:25.480
you say, "I am going to a seminar in Las Vegas for two

00:47:25.500 --> 00:47:28.529
weeks," then they are only going to grant it for two weeks

00:47:28.549 --> 00:47:30.049
possibly. So it really just depends.

00:47:30.049 --> 00:47:31.049
[Audience]

00:47:31.049 --> 00:47:36.960
Well, again, we go back to the some of the initial concepts

00:47:36.980 --> 00:47:39.809
here. The visa, the expiration date on the visa stamp is

00:47:39.829 --> 00:47:41.779
irrelevant once you enter the country. What's relevant is

00:47:41.779 --> 00:47:46.289
the I-94. That determines how long you can stay. So you have

00:47:46.309 --> 00:47:49.309
a B-1 visa stamp that's valid for 10 years but your I-94 -

00:47:49.309 --> 00:47:51.109
but you didn't enter on a B-1, right?

00:47:51.109 --> 00:47:52.109
[Audience]

00:47:51.109 --> 00:47:52.109
[Informal Talk]

00:47:52.109 --> 00:48:02.440
When you come in on the B-1, they would grant it for usually

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:06.940
six months, not allowed to receive salary or compensation

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:09.909
from U.S. source, offer your services for higher. So that's

00:48:09.909 --> 00:48:12.329
another thing that comes up. You start a company, and you

00:48:12.329 --> 00:48:15.329
are starting to do stuff for other companies, and you really

00:48:15.349 --> 00:48:18.880
can't do that, or other activities involving local

00:48:18.900 --> 00:48:22.000
employment.

00:48:22.019 --> 00:48:29.250
Okay. Just real briefly TNs, if you are a Canadian, that's a

00:48:29.269 --> 00:48:32.199
classification that might work. It allows you to skip the

00:48:32.219 --> 00:48:35.829
immigration service. Downsides with that, very limited

00:48:35.849 --> 00:48:39.739
occupational classification, so there is no executive or

00:48:39.759 --> 00:48:41.869
regular management type of position under the TN

00:48:41.889 --> 00:48:45.170
regulations. It's for very specific positions like engineer,

00:48:45.190 --> 00:48:48.309
accountant, lawyers, etc., etc. So oftentimes, it doesn't

00:48:48.329 --> 00:48:50.920
really work for people like you that are starting up a

00:48:50.920 --> 00:48:53.420
company even if you are a Canadian or a Mexican.

00:48:53.420 --> 00:48:57.299
H1B1, again, we touched on this, it's very much like H-1B

00:48:57.319 --> 00:49:02.349
but there is a set-aside for Chile and Singapore. The

00:49:02.369 --> 00:49:06.359
advantages of that, if you qualify, are that first of all,

00:49:06.379 --> 00:49:08.879
it doesn't go through USCIS, you apply directly at the

00:49:08.879 --> 00:49:10.379
consulate or embassy, filing fees are much, much less

00:49:10.379 --> 00:49:11.379
expensive.

00:49:11.379 --> 00:49:18.730
E-3, again, that is also very much like the H-1B in terms of

00:49:18.750 --> 00:49:22.109
what the requirements are but it's not petition based. If

00:49:22.129 --> 00:49:24.630
you are an Australian, you can apply directly at consulate

00:49:24.650 --> 00:49:27.909
or embassy in Australia. There is a separate quota. It's not

00:49:27.929 --> 00:49:34.179
taken out of the regular H-1B cap at $10,000.

00:49:34.199 --> 00:49:37.059
The next classification we will talk about, and I am going

00:49:37.079 --> 00:49:39.829
to keep this brief as well because I don't think it's

00:49:39.829 --> 00:49:42.579
applicable to too many of you, is the L-1A and L-1B

00:49:42.579 --> 00:49:46.269
multinational manager. Let's say you have a company that you

00:49:46.289 --> 00:49:50.799
started or you work for abroad, and you work there for at

00:49:50.819 --> 00:49:55.400
least a year. And when you graduate from Stanford after your

00:49:55.420 --> 00:49:58.659
two-year program, you want to go back and start a company

00:49:58.679 --> 00:50:01.049
here in the United States with that company, a subsidiary

00:50:01.049 --> 00:50:06.500
essentially. The L-1 classification theoretically should

00:50:06.519 --> 00:50:09.710
allow you to do that. If you worked at a company abroad for

00:50:09.710 --> 00:50:12.809
one year in the last three years, of course, I would be

00:50:12.829 --> 00:50:15.579
cutting it very tight because you are going to school for

00:50:15.579 --> 00:50:18.449
two years, you can work for a subsidiary parent or affiliate

00:50:18.469 --> 00:50:23.989
of that foreign company in L-1 status. So just to go back to

00:50:24.009 --> 00:50:27.789
that example, you have a company of maybe 10 people in

00:50:27.809 --> 00:50:31.659
Belgium, and you are coming here for your MBA, when you

00:50:31.679 --> 00:50:34.449
graduate, you are going to start up a subsidiary of that

00:50:34.469 --> 00:50:39.219
company in Belgium here in Palo Alto that makes chocolate or

00:50:39.239 --> 00:50:40.239
something.

00:50:40.239 --> 00:50:44.659
So that just seems like a good option. The problem is this

00:50:44.679 --> 00:50:49.769
that even more so than the H-1B, the USCIS hates L-1 for

00:50:49.789 --> 00:50:53.349
small companies. The chances of a denial for a small company

00:50:53.369 --> 00:50:57.929
on L-1 are extremely high. So we almost never do this for

00:50:57.949 --> 00:51:01.719
startups. We do it where there is no other option or we know

00:51:01.739 --> 00:51:05.089
that we have a fallback. So let's give it a shot, if it

00:51:05.089 --> 00:51:07.589
doesn't work - because there are some advantages of the L-1,

00:51:07.589 --> 00:51:10.839
if you get it, compared to the E-2. If it doesn't work, we

00:51:10.839 --> 00:51:14.339
will do an E-2. That might be a situation where we do the L-

00:51:14.339 --> 00:51:17.269
1. And I just mentioned it so you might hear about it, say,

00:51:17.269 --> 00:51:21.309
"Why don't we do that" if you have that situation. It's

00:51:21.329 --> 00:51:25.929
very, very difficult.

00:51:25.949 --> 00:51:26.949
[Audience]

00:51:26.949 --> 00:51:29.929
You can't unless it's related and unless it meets those

00:51:29.929 --> 00:51:32.609
qualifications. So if you are working for, let's say, a

00:51:32.609 --> 00:51:36.579
subsidiary of the Belgian chocolate company here and then

00:51:36.599 --> 00:51:39.149
you want to switch and work at Google, it's not going to

00:51:39.149 --> 00:51:41.649
work because there is no relationship between Google and the

00:51:41.649 --> 00:51:46.730
Belgian company.

00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:52.029
Okay. I am just going to talk a little bit more about the

00:51:52.049 --> 00:51:57.329
cap-gap issue, and that is the situation where all of you

00:51:57.349 --> 00:52:00.849
might find yourself in where you have your one year of OPT

00:52:00.869 --> 00:52:03.989
and then you need to switch to H-1B. Because of the limited

00:52:03.989 --> 00:52:07.920
filing season for H-1Bs, oftentimes what happens is this

00:52:07.940 --> 00:52:12.119
that when your OPT expires, there is no H-1Bs left. Right

00:52:12.139 --> 00:52:15.869
now, we are looking at H-1Bs for fiscal year 2011 being

00:52:15.889 --> 00:52:19.609
reached in the next couple of weeks, maybe a month or so. So

00:52:19.629 --> 00:52:23.730
if you don't already have an H-1B approved, and you have

00:52:23.750 --> 00:52:29.460
your OPT, once your OPT expires, then you might be in a bad

00:52:29.480 --> 00:52:33.619
situation, depending on when it expires.

00:52:33.639 --> 00:52:38.109
The regulations now allow for what are called "cap-gap

00:52:38.129 --> 00:52:44.569
provisions." This means that if you file an H-1B for the

00:52:44.589 --> 00:52:49.900
next fiscal year, so that would be fiscal year 2012, before

00:52:49.920 --> 00:52:54.109
your OPT expires, then your OPT work authorization will be

00:52:54.129 --> 00:52:56.750
extended till the beginning of the next fiscal year. So

00:52:56.769 --> 00:53:00.480
where this might come into place is let's say you are

00:53:00.500 --> 00:53:05.829
graduating in the spring of this year. Your OPT is going to

00:53:05.849 --> 00:53:12.619
take you till June of 2012. It's 12 months, it will take you

00:53:12.639 --> 00:53:17.900
from the time you graduate till June 2012. You file an H-1B,

00:53:17.920 --> 00:53:21.039
the soonest that you are likely going to able to file an H-

00:53:21.039 --> 00:53:24.969
1B is April 1st, 2012 for the next fiscal year. The problem

00:53:24.989 --> 00:53:28.759
is that before that fiscal year starts on October 1st, your

00:53:28.779 --> 00:53:33.420
OPT will have expired. This cap-gap provision means that you

00:53:33.440 --> 00:53:36.849
can extend your H-1B until the beginning of the fiscal year.

00:53:36.869 --> 00:53:39.619
Does that sort of make sense? Or that just clears mud.

00:53:39.619 --> 00:53:40.619
[Audience]

00:53:40.619 --> 00:53:46.889
Yeah. That applies to you. The other thing is STEM

00:53:46.909 --> 00:53:49.589
extension, which was mentioned, it wouldn't apply to an MBA

00:53:49.589 --> 00:53:52.420
student. If you are graduating in a STEM field; Science,

00:53:52.440 --> 00:53:54.920
Technology Engineering, or Mathematics, you can actually

00:53:54.940 --> 00:53:59.079
extend your OPT for 17 months so long as the employer that

00:53:59.099 --> 00:54:01.920
you are working for is enrolled and E-verified, which I won'

00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:05.889
t go into, and that's regardless of whether an H-1B is

00:54:05.909 --> 00:54:09.559
filed. The cap-gap thing, which is applicable to you, only

00:54:09.579 --> 00:54:14.359
applies if you file an H-1B within the requisite time

00:54:14.379 --> 00:54:19.539
period. So that's just a very short summary of that. But if

00:54:19.559 --> 00:54:25.420
you have questions about it, feel free to contact me.

00:54:25.440 --> 00:54:28.440
Green Cards, again, I don't think this is too relevant to

00:54:28.449 --> 00:54:30.879
you now because right now, you are probably just thinking

00:54:30.879 --> 00:54:35.619
about getting a work visa once you graduate. But just to

00:54:35.639 --> 00:54:39.659
give a broad overview of some of what might be relevant

00:54:39.679 --> 00:54:43.289
classifications and considerations, one issue for startups

00:54:43.309 --> 00:54:46.269
is financial resources. Whenever you do an employer-

00:54:46.289 --> 00:54:49.129
sponsored Green Card case, you have to be able to show the

00:54:49.129 --> 00:54:53.299
company's ability to pay the salary. So if you are saying

00:54:53.319 --> 00:54:56.569
that you are going to make $100,000, they are going to want

00:54:56.569 --> 00:54:59.569
to see proof that you can do that with either tax returns,

00:54:59.569 --> 00:55:01.329
financial statements or payroll records. And obviously,

00:55:01.349 --> 00:55:05.759
that's difficult for startups.

00:55:05.779 --> 00:55:08.529
Immigrant intent, that might come into play. Some people

00:55:08.549 --> 00:55:11.549
want to start the Green Card process with a startup or it's

00:55:11.549 --> 00:55:14.710
some other classification while they are on F-1 OPT status.

00:55:14.730 --> 00:55:17.250
That's difficult because the F-1 OPT is a strict

00:55:17.250 --> 00:55:21.319
nonimmigrant status, and you could face problems traveling

00:55:21.339 --> 00:55:23.809
and reentering the country if you are starting the Green

00:55:23.809 --> 00:55:26.960
Card process. Before embarking on the Green Card process,

00:55:26.980 --> 00:55:30.699
you should talk to a tax advisor. There are implications in

00:55:30.719 --> 00:55:34.369
terms of taxes.

00:55:34.389 --> 00:55:40.019
And now, just real briefly, means of sponsorship. Most

00:55:40.039 --> 00:55:43.940
people who are employer sponsored as Green Card holders do

00:55:43.960 --> 00:55:47.509
so through PERM Labor Certification and that's a process

00:55:47.529 --> 00:55:50.460
where the employer tests the U.S. labor market to determine

00:55:50.480 --> 00:55:53.559
whether there is any qualified U.S. workers who want the

00:55:53.579 --> 00:55:56.779
job. And of course, the intention here is to make sure that

00:55:56.799 --> 00:55:59.799
no U.S. workers have been displaced to protect the U.S.

00:55:59.799 --> 00:56:03.710
workers. What that means for startups is that is difficult

00:56:03.730 --> 00:56:06.980
because as a startup, if you are a founder or a co-founder,

00:56:07.000 --> 00:56:09.500
you are probably going to have some ownership interest in

00:56:09.500 --> 00:56:12.250
the company. And if you have an ownership interest in the

00:56:12.250 --> 00:56:15.019
company, and it's what's considered a closely held company,

00:56:15.039 --> 00:56:18.099
which a lot of startups are, the Department of Labor is

00:56:18.119 --> 00:56:21.119
going to want you to disclose that when you file a PERM

00:56:21.119 --> 00:56:23.230
application, and it's going to be very, very difficult to

00:56:23.230 --> 00:56:26.809
get that approved. So that's a very important consideration

00:56:26.829 --> 00:56:29.279
because the other means of Green Card sponsorship are very

00:56:29.279 --> 00:56:30.839
difficult.

00:56:30.859 --> 00:56:34.009
When the company gets to a certain level, and it gets

00:56:34.029 --> 00:56:37.519
diluted with venture capital, we might be at the point where

00:56:37.539 --> 00:56:40.289
we could say well, it's not closely held anymore. But while

00:56:40.289 --> 00:56:44.409
it's closely held, I wouldn't even want to file a PERM

00:56:44.429 --> 00:56:49.719
application for someone that owns 5% or so of the company.

00:56:49.739 --> 00:56:52.980
EB1, alien of extraordinary ability, this is very similar to

00:56:53.000 --> 00:56:55.859
the O-1. It's just the standard of review is much higher. So

00:56:55.859 --> 00:56:58.859
this is for people that are very top at their field. This

00:56:58.859 --> 00:57:02.759
might be an executive who has done well and made lots of

00:57:02.779 --> 00:57:06.569
money with previous companies. It's also done for actors and

00:57:06.589 --> 00:57:13.279
performers and musicians and other things. But that's a hard

00:57:13.299 --> 00:57:16.879
standard to meet, particularly in the business field. It's

00:57:16.899 --> 00:57:20.869
much easier for someone who has got a PhD or they have a

00:57:20.889 --> 00:57:23.589
Nobel Prize or something like that. If you have a Nobel

00:57:23.589 --> 00:57:29.089
Prize, by all means, let me know.

00:57:29.109 --> 00:57:31.730
Multinational managers and executives, this is very similar

00:57:31.750 --> 00:57:35.179
to the L-1, the L-1 classification that we just briefly

00:57:35.199 --> 00:57:39.210
touched on. So if you have a company abroad that you are

00:57:39.230 --> 00:57:42.440
coming here, and when you graduate, you are going to open a

00:57:42.460 --> 00:57:45.210
subsidiary or you are going to join a subsidiary, and you

00:57:45.210 --> 00:57:48.789
are going to work in a managerial capacity or an executive

00:57:48.809 --> 00:57:52.159
capacity at that company in the U.S. and you served in a

00:57:52.179 --> 00:57:55.170
managerial executive capacity at the company abroad, then

00:57:55.190 --> 00:57:59.069
you should qualify as a multinational manager executive. But

00:57:59.089 --> 00:58:02.460
again, for small companies, that's difficult. It's difficult

00:58:02.480 --> 00:58:05.269
to show that because if you only have four-five employees,

00:58:05.289 --> 00:58:08.609
and you are saying that you are the CEO, the immigration

00:58:08.629 --> 00:58:12.389
service is going to say, "the CEO of what." They want to see

00:58:12.409 --> 00:58:16.199
big companies with lots of employees.

00:58:16.219 --> 00:58:20.319
And finally, the EB-5 Investor Visa, and you might have

00:58:20.339 --> 00:58:25.049
heard of this, but this is a Green Card visa classification

00:58:25.069 --> 00:58:28.639
that allows for people to get a Green Card if they are

00:58:28.659 --> 00:58:32.089
investing $1 million, and in some cases, $0.5 million

00:58:32.109 --> 00:58:36.929
depending on if it's in a targeted economic area, to start a

00:58:36.949 --> 00:58:39.899
business, it creates at least 10 jobs. And there is a two-

00:58:39.899 --> 00:58:42.449
year conditional residency requirement. So what you have to

00:58:42.469 --> 00:58:45.949
do is you have to take the money, start the business, start

00:58:45.969 --> 00:58:50.799
buying stuff and get space and all that, and then you file a

00:58:50.819 --> 00:58:53.819
petition saying, "Oh, yeah, here is my business, here is my

00:58:53.839 --> 00:58:57.089
business plan. I put in the money. It's legal, I have got it

00:58:57.089 --> 00:58:59.529
from legal means." And then they give you a two-year

00:58:59.529 --> 00:59:02.539
conditional Green Card. And at the end of that two years,

00:59:02.559 --> 00:59:06.399
you have to show that you in fact did or are likely going to

00:59:06.419 --> 00:59:09.519
create the 10 jobs that you are required to, and that the

00:59:09.539 --> 00:59:11.769
business is still running. Now, important aspect of this, it

00:59:11.769 --> 00:59:15.170
has to be your money. You can't use someone else's money;

00:59:15.190 --> 00:59:18.440
you can't use VC funding. It has to be your own money. And

00:59:18.440 --> 00:59:21.690
they are going to trace it, they can go back five years to

00:59:21.690 --> 00:59:24.940
make sure that that's your money and you got it from a legal

00:59:24.940 --> 00:59:30.420
means.

00:59:30.440 --> 00:59:31.440
[Audience]

00:59:31.440 --> 00:59:34.940
Yes. If you get your Green Card through Google, then you are

00:59:34.960 --> 00:59:37.909
a permanent resident, you can do whatever you want, or if

00:59:37.929 --> 00:59:44.980
you get your Green Card through marriage.

00:59:45.000 --> 00:59:46.000
[Audience]

00:59:46.000 --> 00:59:48.480
It depends on your background. Right now, it's going very

00:59:48.480 --> 00:59:52.299
quickly through PERM Labor Certification. It also depends on

00:59:52.319 --> 00:59:55.210
if the PERM is audited. In some cases, they are audited. In

00:59:55.210 --> 00:59:59.379
that case, it takes much longer. It depends on your academic

00:59:59.399 --> 01:00:02.239
background. It depends on your nationality - or actually not

01:00:02.259 --> 01:00:04.949
your nationality, excuse me - the country that you are born

01:00:04.969 --> 01:00:08.179
in. And in some cases, it might depend on the country your

01:00:08.199 --> 01:00:11.739
wife was born in, or your husband. So there is a lot of

01:00:11.759 --> 01:00:14.029
factors. But just to give you an example, someone that's

01:00:14.029 --> 01:00:19.049
working for Google or some other established company, they

01:00:19.069 --> 01:00:21.849
have a Masters degree, they are in a position that requires

01:00:21.869 --> 01:00:24.659
a Masters degree, and they are born in Canada, and Google

01:00:24.679 --> 01:00:27.739
starts the PERM process, it might only take three or four

01:00:27.759 --> 01:00:31.170
months to get the PERM taken care of based on current

01:00:31.190 --> 01:00:33.339
processing times. And then the rest of the process may be

01:00:33.339 --> 01:00:37.259
six or nine months. So the best case scenario is a Green

01:00:37.279 --> 01:00:39.489
Card in about a year.

01:00:39.509 --> 01:00:44.440
Worst case scenario, you are born in India, you are in a

01:00:44.460 --> 01:00:47.480
position that only requires a bachelor's degree, that might

01:00:47.500 --> 01:00:48.750
be eight or nine years.

01:00:48.759 --> 01:00:49.759
[Audience]

01:00:49.759 --> 01:00:59.869
Usually, most companies, most of my clients, they wait a

01:00:59.889 --> 01:01:02.879
year. They want to see you are working for a year. Startups

01:01:02.879 --> 01:01:05.289
might have more flexibility. They want to track the best

01:01:05.289 --> 01:01:08.289
talent. So they will do it right away for you. But most

01:01:08.289 --> 01:01:09.789
companies, they have a one-year policy.

01:01:09.789 --> 01:01:13.159
Okay, I know we are out of time but I am just going to

01:01:13.159 --> 01:01:16.000
briefly mention this and then we can move to the other room,

01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:18.750
the EB-6 Green Card category, that was a proposed law. It

01:01:18.750 --> 01:01:21.500
was started last year. You might have heard about it. This

01:01:21.500 --> 01:01:25.079
would allow people like you to get a Green Card by raising

01:01:25.099 --> 01:01:28.309
VC funding. So unlike the EB-5 which requires it to be your

01:01:28.309 --> 01:01:31.309
own money, the EB-6 will allow you to raise VC funding to

01:01:31.309 --> 01:01:35.619
get a Green Card that way. Well, the law didn't go anywhere.

01:01:35.639 --> 01:01:42.019
So unfortunately, it failed and Congress didn't pass it. But

01:01:42.039 --> 01:01:43.989
it's possible that it would be reintroduced in the new

01:01:43.989 --> 01:02:05.199
session of Congress, and it might go somewhere.

